RS.14 wrote:
> I was playing a barbarian a few days ago (no longer--killed by
> Demogorgon), but I realized that, with my boots of speed, I could
> simply wait for cockatrices to step next to me; hit them, step away,
> and repeat until they were dead. There doesn't seem to be any risk:
> they couldn't hit me at all. Why have I never heard about this? Does
> anyone else use this?
> BTW, I tried in wiz mode; there is some risk without you're speed
> boosted in some way, but it seems very small.

Wow... I couldn't really believe this when I read the post, but a brief
check of the spoilers reveals that a cockatrice's speed is only 6,
while your speed with speed boots (in non-polymorphed form) is 18.  So
no, there wouldn't be any risk, as you'll get an average of three moves
to the cockatrice's one.  At the very least, you'll get two consecutive
moves to the cockatrice's one, time enough to hit and step away.

Why then isn't this a more commonly discussed strategy?  I imagine
that's because it's so risky.  As explained above, yes, it should work,
but what if
1. the cockatrice gets hit by a wand/potion(?) of speed monster/speed?
2. you step on a polymorph trap and are polymorphed into some slow
monster?
3. you autopickup a heavy item and become burdened? (Okay, I'm reaching
here - I'm sure everyone on this NG has their autopickup preferences
under control.)
4. etc.
?  What I'm saying is that there are a lot of things that could
(theoretically) go wrong.  I'd submit that you don't want to be moving
when fighting a cockatrice if you can help it, and that the speed boots
method should be considered a *distant* second to the traditional
stand-far-away-and-throw-whatever-you've-got method.

Of course, there's a time for everything, and I'm glad RS.14 brought up
this method of dealing with footrices.

